      "id": 1,
      "text": "Before settlers arrived, the Cherokee used the Allred area as a hunting ground and kept small villages in the Upper Cumberland region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/allred_tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Allred takes its name from Jonathan Allred, a North Carolina pioneer who settled the land in the early 1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/allred_tennessee",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Jonathan and his relative Theophilus Allred established homesteads along the West Fork River, and the community grew around their land claims.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/allred_tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1904 opening of the Overton County Railroad linked Allred to Livingston and Algood, making travel and trade far easier.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/allred_tennessee",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Allred’s low‑density rural setting encourages sustainable forestry and agriculture, reflecting the community’s long‑standing connection to the forested terrain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
